### What is HTTP Parameter Pollution?

When a URL contains `?role=user&role=admin`, there is no universal rule in the HTTP specification for which value wins. Each technology makes its own choice. This inconsistency is the vulnerability.

```text
// The ambiguous request:
GET /api/v1/transfer?accountType=savings&accountType=current

// Java Servlet getParameter("accountType") → "savings" (first)
// Java Servlet getParameterValues("accountType") → ["savings", "current"]
// Spring @RequestParam String accountType → "savings" (first)
// Spring @RequestParam List<String> accountType → ["savings", "current"]
// Some gateways → "current" (last)
```

### How does the attack work?

The attacker relies on the security layer and the business layer reading different occurrences. The security check sees the safe value and allows the request. The business logic reads the dangerous value and acts on it.

```java
// Scenario: gateway reads LAST value, backend reads FIRST value
// Attacker sends: ?role=admin&role=user

// Gateway authorization check (reads last): "user" → allowed
// Backend service (reads first): "admin" → executes with admin privileges!
```

### How do you reject duplicate parameters?

The strongest fix is to reject any single-valued parameter that appears more than once. Return 400 Bad Request before any business logic runs.

```java
package com.bank.filter;

import jakarta.servlet.*;
import jakarta.servlet.http.*;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.Map;

public class HppProtectionFilter implements Filter {

    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest req, ServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)
            throws IOException, ServletException {
        HttpServletRequest request = (HttpServletRequest) req;
        HttpServletResponse response = (HttpServletResponse) res;

        for (Map.Entry<String, String[]> entry : request.getParameterMap().entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue().length > 1) {
                response.setStatus(400);
                response.getWriter().write(
                        "{\"error\":\"Duplicate parameter not allowed: "
                        + entry.getKey() + "\"}");
                return;
            }
        }
        chain.doFilter(req, res);
    }
}
```

### How does canonicalization work?

If you must accept duplicates (for example, a multi-select filter), collapse them to a single deterministic value. Pick first-wins or last-wins, document it, and make every layer use the same rule.

```java
// Canonicalize to first-wins for single-valued params:
String role = request.getParameterValues("role")[0]; // always first
// Document: "For single-valued parameters, this API uses first-wins semantics."
```

The danger is not duplicates themselves; it is different layers choosing differently.

### How does strict schema validation help?

Validate the request against a strict typed DTO. When Spring binds `@RequestParam AccountType accountType` to an enum, a polluted parameter fails binding cleanly.

```java
package com.bank.dto;

public enum AccountType { SAVINGS, CURRENT }

// Controller with strict enum binding:
@GetMapping("/api/v1/transfer")
public TransferResponse transfer(
        @RequestParam AccountType accountType,  // enum binding
        @RequestParam @DecimalMin("0.01") BigDecimal amount) {
    // If ?accountType=savings&accountType=current is sent,
    // Spring fails binding to the single enum → 400 Bad Request
    return transferService.execute(accountType, amount);
}
```

### What about HPP in forwarded requests?

HPP also appears in query strings forwarded to downstream services. If your service builds a downstream URL by concatenating user input, an attacker can inject `&admin=true` through a polluted parameter.

```java
// Wrong – concatenating user input into a downstream URL:
String url = "http://internal/api?type=" + request.getParameter("type");
// Attacker sends: ?type=regular&admin=true
// Downstream URL becomes: http://internal/api?type=regular&admin=true

// Correct – whitelist and URL-encode:
String type = validateAgainstWhitelist(request.getParameter("type"));
String url = "http://internal/api?type=" + URLEncoder.encode(type, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is not checking for duplicate parameters at all. The second mistake is having the gateway and the backend use different first-vs-last semantics without realizing it. The third mistake is allowing multi-valued binding for parameters that should be single-valued.

```java
// Wrong – accepts a list for a single-valued param:
@RequestParam List<String> role  // ["user", "admin"] – which one wins?

// Correct – accepts a single value:
@RequestParam String role  // Spring takes the first; filter rejects duplicates
```

### How do you test for HPP vulnerabilities?

Send requests with duplicate parameters and verify the API rejects them or handles them consistently.

```bash
# Test: send duplicate accountType parameter
curl "https://api.bank.com/api/v1/transfer?accountType=savings&accountType=current&amount=50000"
# Expected: 400 Bad Request with error message
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Ambiguity is the vulnerability. Any time two components can interpret the same input differently, an attacker will engineer that difference. The remediation is to eliminate the ambiguity: reject duplicates, validate at the boundary with typed schemas, and make sure the gateway and the service speak with one voice about every parameter. In a banking API where accountType determines transfer limits, this is not an academic concern; it is a real authorization bypass vector.
